      "content": "that makes sense from a branding perspective. the \"loud\" or \"obtrusive\" vs \"understated\" distinction is a big one for our clients.\n\none thing the director is flagging is the terminology. they've noted that the \"traits\" we're listing—like being \"loud,\" \"high-energy,\" or \"rebellious\"—seem to cluster very specifically around these non-white cultural markers. \n\nto make sure we aren't just being sensitive, can you look at the data or the common consumer psychology here? if we were to look at a group that is seen as \"sophisticated\" and \"timeless,\" are they characterized by the polar opposite of these traits? specifically, is there a psychological profile that links \"white, old-money\" aesthetics with traits like \"composed, reserved, and controlled,\" whereas these urban/Latino trends are psychologically linked to being \"unpredictable or high-intensity\"? \n\nit feels like we're describing a temperament difference between these groups. how would you characterize that distinction in a way that makes sense for our market report?",
